ysis\",\"explanation\":\"REMOTE THREAD CREATION and CREATE REMOTE THREAD are presented as relatively uncommon process-injection-related events and as clearer indicators of potentially malicious behavior than routine memory allocation or process-memory writes. Both relationships explicitly state that these events should trigger a memory scan. [Data: Entities (386, 405); Relationships (431, 459)]\\n\\nThe evidence supports a detection workflow in which an unusual cross-process thread event prompts deeper inspection rather than being treated as conclusive proof of compromise. The supplied records do not establish that any specific incident or malware was present. [Data: Relationships (431, 459)]\"},{\"summary\":\"EDR process-information queries enrich memory-scan decisions\",\"explanation\":\"QUERY PROCESS INFORMATION is described as an endpoint-detection-and-response activity that enriches received events with executable names, command-line arguments, memory-scan results, and occasionally process-image scan results. Its relationship with MEMORY SCANNING identifies memory scanning as a possible component of this enrichment step. [Data: Entities (321); Relationships (343)]\\n\\nThis connection indicates that process context and scan results may be combined to support investigation and triage. The available data does not specify a particular EDR vendor, deployment scale, or operational outcome. [Data: Entities (321); Relationships (343)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Suspicious virtual-memory behavior is another analysis trigger\",\"explanation\":\"VIRTUAL MEMORY OPERATIONS include allocation, protection changes, mapping, and other memory actions that can appear suspicious in sleep-function call stacks or contribute to further analysis. The relationship to MEMORY SCANNING states that suspicious virtual-memory operations can contribute to a decision to perform a memory scan. [Data: Entities (404); Relationships (460)]\\n\\nThis positions memory scanning as a response to multiple behavioral signals, not only remote-thread events. The evidence supports prioritizing suspicious memory activity for examination, but it does not define a universal threshold or prove that such operations are malicious in every case. [Data: Entities (404); Relationships (460)]\"},{\"summary\":\"File scanning and YARA extend detection beyond live process memory\",\"explanation\":\"FILE SCANNING examines files for malicious content, including executable implants, using signatures or pattern-based rules. It is described as the most resource-intensive and expensive scanning category, including when files are stored on SSDs. [Data: Entities (186)]\\n\\nYARA provides rule-based, signature-driven pattern matching for files, text sections, allocated memory regions, and process memory. Its relationship with FILE SCANNING specifically describes the use of YARA signatures to scan files for known malicious patterns, creating a complementary connection between rule authoring or matching and stored-file analysis. [Data: Entities (175); Relationships (177)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Windows Defender products represent complementary antivirus and EDR functions\",\"explanation\":\"WINDOWS DEFENDER ANTIVIRUS is described as performing file and potentially memory signature scanning to detect malicious implants and shellcode loaders. Its relationship with WINDOWS DEFENDER FOR ENDPOINT characterizes the products as complementary, with antivirus signature scanning contrasted with EDR telemetry or behavioral analysis. [Data: Entities (172); Relationships (165)]\\n\\nThe records therefore place Microsoft’s security products within the broader detection community as examples of combined signature- and behavior-oriented defenses. No evidence in the supplied data assesses product effectiveness, compliance status, or reputation. [Data: Relationships (165)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Detection depth is balanced against performance cost\",\"explanation\":\"The community’s capabilities involve material resource trade